BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Charlie Musselwhite
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260619T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260619T000000
UID:2603258BMztZPyfkGkDMepgIraag
DESCRIPTION:Harmonica master Charlie Musselwhite’s personal history is t
 he kind of tale a novelist would sell his soul for. He is a larger-than-li
 fe musical legend\, writing and singing what he calls\, “music from the 
 heart.” His life reads like a classic blues song: born in Mississippi\, 
 raised in Memphis and schooled on the South Side of Chicago. A groundbreak
 ing recording artist since the 1960s\, Musselwhite continues to create tra
 ilblazing music while remaining firmly rooted in the blues. His worldly-wi
 se vocals\, rich\, melodic harmonica playing \\(and his deep country blues
  guitar work\\) flawlessly accompany his often autobiographical and always
  memorable original songs. Living Blues says\, “Musselwhite’s rock-sol
 id vocals creep up and overwhelm you before you know it. He plays magnific
 ent harp with superb dexterity and phrasing. The results are amazing.”\n
 \nBorn into a blue collar family in Kosciusko\, Mississippi on January 31\
 , 1944 and raised by a single mother\, Musselwhite grew up surrounded by b
 lues\, hillbilly and gospel music on the radio and around performing music
 ians in his neighborhood. His family moved to Memphis\, where\, as a teena
 ger\, he worked as a ditch digger\, concrete layer and moonshine runner. F
 ascinated by the blues\, Musselwhite began playing guitar and harmonica. A
 s a teen\, Musselwhite hobnobbed with many of the local musicians\, includ
 ing Elvis Presley\, Johnny Cash and Johnny Burnette\, but the celebrities 
 young Charlie sought out were Memphis’ veteran bluesmen like Furry Lewis
 \, Will Shade and Gus Cannon.\n\nFollowing the path of so many\, Musselwhi
 te moved to Chicago looking for better paying work. While driving an exter
 minator truck as a day job\, Charlie lived on the South Side and hung out 
 in blues clubs at night\, developing close friendships with blues giants B
 ig Walter Horton and Big Joe Williams\, who became his key mentors. He roo
 med with Williams\, and was also close to Little Walter\, Sonny Boy Willia
 mson and Howlin’ Wolf\, always soaking up licks and lyrics\, and learnin
 g how to be a bluesman. Before long\, he was sitting in at clubs with Mudd
 y Waters\, Magic Sam\, Earl Hooker\, Jimmy Reed\, Luther Allison and other
 s\, building an impressive local word-of-mouth reputation.\n\nAfter the re
 lease of his first full-length LP on Vanguard—Stand Back! Here Comes Cha
 rley Musselwhite’s South Side Band—he was embraced by the growing yout
 h counter-culture. His subsequent releases found a place with the newly em
 erging progressive rock FM radio stations\, especially on the West Coast. 
 His iconic status established\, he relocated to San Francisco\, often play
 ing the famed Fillmore Auditorium. Over the years\, Charlie has released n
 early 40 albums on a variety of labels. Musically\, his albums range from 
 straight blues to mixing elements of jazz\, gospel\, Tex-Mex\, Cuban and o
 ther world music. Four of those albums—1990’s Ace Of Harps\, 1991’s 
 Signature\, 1994’s In My Time\, and 2010’s The Well—were recorded fo
 r Alligator Records and remain among his best-selling titles.\n\nIn additi
 on to his own recordings\, Musselwhite has guested on numerous recordings\
 , as a featured player with Tom Waits\, Eddie Vedder\, Ben Harper\, John L
 ee Hooker\, Bonnie Raitt\, The Blind Boys of Alabama\, INXS\, Cyndi Lauper
 \, and many others. He was inducted into the Blues Foundation’s Blues Ha
 ll Of Fame in 2010\, has been nominated for eleven Grammy Awards \\(winnin
 g one\\) and has won many Living Blues Awards and Blues Music Awards.
LOCATION:HopMonk Tavern Novato
URL:https://www.bandsintown.com/e/1037796823?app_id=pkvts0on0m&came_from=2
 87&utm_medium=api&utm_source=partner_api&utm_campaign=event
X-SOURCE:Press Democrat
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Sonoma AI - Agents & Tiny AI
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260619T160000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260619T190000
UID:260617VdVrXfYN_k2EGRCsq7sGwg
DESCRIPTION:This is an in-person event! Registration is required in order 
 to get the address and get in.\n\n​**Topic:** Sonoma AI Meetups for rem
 ote tech workers\, to build a North Bay AI community\, collaboratively lea
 rn\, share ideas\, and network with others who are building the future of 
 AI.\n\n​**What we’ll do:** Meet & learn about AI tools\, AI infrastruc
 ture\, and AI best practices - with food + wine.\n\n​**Tech Talk 1:** Y
 our AI Pair Programmer Hates Your Tech Debt Too **Speaker**: Shagun Bhatia
 \, Atlassian **Abstract**: Tech debt is a problem every engineering team k
 nows is real\, but rarely has the time or clarity to tackle systematically
 . It compounds silently - slowing down feature work\, increasing bug rates
 \, and making onboarding painful - until it's too expensive to ignore. In 
 this talk\, I'll walk through how we built an AI-assisted tech debt workfl
 ow at Atlassian that goes far beyond static analysis.\n\n​**Tech Talk 2:
 ** It's Not the Model. It's What You Fed It. **Speaker**: Zayne Turner\, 
 Workato **Abstract**: When your AI agent fails\, the instinct is to blame 
 the model: try a different one\, adjust the prompt\, add more context. But
  most agent failures aren't model problems. They're design problems. This 
 talk looks at what happens inside an agent at runtime\, why naive tool des
 ign compounds failure\, and three principles for improving agent performan
 ce — drawn from an open-source hotel operations system built to demonstr
 ate the patterns at production scale.\n\n​**Tech Talk 3:** AI Wants to 
 Be Local: Beating the Cloud on 30MB of RAM **Speaker**: Pete Warden\, Moon
 shine AI **Abstract**: Today many people assume that cutting-edge AI requi
 res massive data centers. But if you’ve ever used a voice interface that
  takes two full seconds to respond\, you know the cloud is failing the use
 r experience. In this talk\, you’ll learn how rethinking transformer arc
 hitectures with variable input windows and incremental streaming cache can
  drop latency to near-instantaneous levels and shrink RAM footprints down 
 to less than 30MB. Discover why the future of AI isn't in the cloud\, but 
 right on your device\, completely private and offline.\n\n​**Who Should 
 attend:** Anyone interested in talking and learning about developing Agent
 ic AI Apps.\n\n​**Where:** This is an in-person event. Registration is r
 equired to get the address and into the event. Registration in advance wil
 l close 2 days before the event.
LOCATION:Sebastopol Library
URL:https://luma.com/jz1s0dbj
X-SOURCE:Press Democrat
END:VEVENT
